cs=[claim('11',1,[p1],'Pressure expresses how much perpendicular force acts on each unit of contact area.','definition'),claim('11',2,[p2],'For a uniformly loaded surface, pressure equals perpendicular force divided by area: P = F/A. The pascal is a newton per square metre.','formula'),claim('11',3,[p3],'With the same perpendicular force, a larger contact area produces a smaller pressure.','causal'),claim('11',4,[p2],'One square centimetre is one ten-thousandth of a square metre because both length dimensions are converted.','factual')];cid=[c['claim_id'] for c in cs]
bs=[block('11',1,'learner_objective','What you will learn','Define pressure, calculate it in pascals and explain the effect of changing contact area.',[],['A']),block('11',2,'definition','Force spread over an area','A force can be distributed over a small or large surface. Pressure compares the perpendicular force with the contact area over which it acts. It describes force per area, rather than force alone. The pressure can therefore change even when the force stays fixed. The force component used is perpendicular to the surface: a sideways force must not simply be inserted as though it were a normal load.',[cid[0]],['A']),block('11',3,'formula','The pressure relation','Use P = F/A for a load spread uniformly across area A. Here F is the perpendicular force in newtons, A is the area in square metres and P is the pressure in pascals. A pressure of one pascal corresponds to one newton acting on each square metre. Write the relation before inserting the values. If the requested quantity is force, rearrange it to F = PA; if it is area, use A = F/P. The units help check the rearrangement: force divided by pressure has units of area.',[cid[1]],['A','B']),block('11',4,'worked_calculation','Worked example: a model press','A classroom model exerts 48 N uniformly over a contact area of 0.0060 m². Use P = F/A. Substitution gives 48 ÷ 0.0060 = 8000 Pa. This is 8.0 kPa to two significant figures. Keep the unrounded value during working, then choose a final precision consistent with the supplied measurements. The force and area are invented practice values, not measurements from a published experiment.',[cid[1]],['B']),block('11',5,'plain_explanation','Changing area','Imagine replacing the model press foot with a wider foot while leaving its perpendicular force unchanged. That force is now shared over more square metres, so the force acting on each square metre decreases. The pressure is lower. If area doubles while force is fixed, pressure becomes half as large. That conclusion depends on holding the force fixed; changing force at the same time requires a fresh calculation.',[cid[2]],['A']),block('11',6,'worked_calculation','Converting an area','An area of 12 cm² converts to 0.0012 m². Both dimensions change when an area is converted: a centimetre is one hundredth of a metre, so a square centimetre is one ten-thousandth of a square metre. Convert the area first, then substitute. Dividing an area by one hundred instead of ten thousand introduces a factor-of-one-hundred error. A sketch of a square with both sides labelled can make the squared conversion easier to see.',[cid[3]],['B']),block('11',7,'cross_link','Connections','The units and powers-of-ten skills in topic 1 support pressure calculations. Topic 11 later applies pressure to liquids and hydraulic systems, where identifying which force and which area belong together remains essential.',[],['A'])]
its=[item('11',1,[p1],[cid[0]],'DEF',['A'],'Define',1,'Define pressure.','Pressure is perpendicular force per contact area.',['1 mark: the definition as perpendicular force per area.']),item('11',2,[p2],[cid[1]],'CALC',['B'],'Calculate',2,'A model press spreads a perpendicular force of 48 N uniformly over 0.0060 m². Calculate its pressure in pascals.','P = F/A. With the given values, 48 ÷ 0.0060 = 8000 Pa.',['Equation and substitution: use the force divided by the stated area, 1 mark.','Final answer with unit: 8000 Pa, 1 mark.'],bloom='Apply'),item('11',3,[p3],[cid[2]],'MECH',['A'],'Explain',2,'A flat support is replaced with one having twice the contact area. The perpendicular force stays fixed. Explain the change in pressure.','The force is shared over twice the area, so the pressure falls to half its earlier value.',['Cause: the same force acts over twice the area, 1 mark.','Effect: pressure halves because force per area is halved, 1 mark.']),item('11',4,[p2],[cid[1],cid[3]],'CALC',['B'],'Calculate',3,'A small model clamp exerts 36 N uniformly across 12 cm². Calculate its pressure in pascals. Show the area conversion.','The area is 0.0012 m². P = F/A; 36 ÷ 0.0012 = 30000 Pa.',['Conversion working: 12 cm² becomes 0.0012 m², 1 mark.','Equation and substitution: divide 36 N by the converted area, 1 mark.','Final answer with unit: 30000 Pa, 1 mark.'],bloom='Apply'),item('11',5,[p1,p2,p3],cid,'FEATURE',['A','B'],None,3,'Choose one option for each original practice question.\n1. Which unit measures pressure? A Pa; B N; C m²; D J.\n2. A 20 N perpendicular force acts uniformly on 0.010 m². What is the pressure? A 0.0005 Pa; B 0.20 Pa; C 200 Pa; D 2000 Pa.\n3. At fixed force, doubling the area makes pressure: A double; B halve; C stay fixed; D become zero.','Key: 1 A, 2 D, 3 B. For the pressure question, 20 ÷ 0.010 = 2000 Pa. In question 1, N measures force, m² area and J energy. In question 2, the alternatives come from reversing the ratio, multiplying instead of dividing, or misplacing a zero. In question 3, the wrong options reverse the area effect, ignore it or remove a nonzero force without justification.',['One mark per correct option; key A, D, B.','Explain each distractor through the quantity or calculation error it represents.'],kind='multiple_choice_set',bloom='Apply')]
