bs3=[block('3',1,'learner_objective','What you will learn','Calculate a graph gradient with its unit and support a conclusion using the plotted data.',[],['C']),block('3',2,'formula','Reading what a gradient means','A gradient compares a change on the vertical axis with a change on the horizontal axis. Subtract the two vertical coordinates, subtract the two horizontal coordinates in the same order, then divide. The sign matters. A descending line has a negative gradient when the horizontal variable increases to the right. The name of a physical quantity must follow the axes: a gradient is not automatically density, stiffness or speed.',[c3[0]],['C']),block('3',3,'process','Using the fitted line','Plot the readings, choose suitable scales and draw a line that represents their trend. For a straight line, mark a large triangle on the fitted line. Choose separated locations so that small reading errors have less influence. Use the line itself, not a pair of scattered experimental points that happen to be convenient. Label the coordinates used so that another reader can follow the calculation. The unit is obtained by dividing the vertical-axis unit by the horizontal-axis unit.',[c3[1]],['C']),block('3',4,'worked_calculation','An original practice data set','These values are invented for graph practice. A model trolley has positions of 2, 6, 10, 14, 18 and 22 cm at times of 0, 1, 2, 3, 4 and 5 s respectively. Plot position on the vertical axis and time on the horizontal axis. Between the fitted points at zero and five seconds, the position change is 20 cm and the time change is 5 s. The quotient 20 ÷ 5 = 4 gives a gradient of 4 cm/s. In this context the slope describes the rate of position change.',[c3[0],c3[1]],['C']),block('3',5,'interpretation','A conclusion supported by values','The position increases by 4 cm during each successive second. This supports a constant rate of position change over the interval shown. It does not show that position is directly proportional to elapsed time measured from this zero, because the position begins at 2 cm. A straight trend and a proportional relationship are different statements. A useful conclusion states which is supported and points to the intercept or the repeated equal changes.',[c3[2]],['C']),block('3',6,'cross_link','Connections','Use topic 1 to check units and subtraction. Apply the same graphical method to extension versus load, volume versus mass and the motion graphs in topic 5. The calculation process transfers, but the physical meaning of its result changes with the plotted quantities.',[],['C'])]
i3=[item('3',1,[g1],c3[:2],'CALC',['C'],'Calculate',2,'An invented position–time line passes through (0 s, 2 cm) and (5 s, 22 cm). Calculate its gradient and give its unit.','The vertical change is 20 cm and the horizontal change is 5 s. Gradient = 20 ÷ 5 = 4 cm/s.',['Equation and substitution working: use vertical change divided by horizontal change, 1 mark.','Answer: 4 cm/s with the axis-ratio unit, 1 mark.'],bloom='Apply'),item('3',2,[g2],[c3[2]],'EVAL',['C'],'Explain',2,'A straight position–time line begins at 2 cm when time is zero. Explain whether position is directly proportional to this elapsed time.','It is not directly proportional to this elapsed time because its line misses the origin; the 2 cm intercept is evidence.',['Conclusion: no direct proportionality to the stated time, 1 mark.','Evidence: use the nonzero position at zero time, 1 mark.'],bloom='Evaluate'),item('3',3,[g2],[c3[2]],'PROC',['C'],'Describe',1,'A set of trolley positions is recorded at one-second intervals. Describe one comparison that could support a constant-speed conclusion.','Compare successive changes in position across the equal time intervals; approximately equal changes support a constant rate.',['One method step: compare position changes during equal time intervals, 1 mark.'],bloom='Apply'),item('3',4,[g1,g2],c3,None,['C'],'Draw',10,'The following readings are invented for practice.\n\n| Time / s | Position / cm |\n|---:|---:|\n| 0 | 2 |\n| 1 | 6 |\n| 2 | 10 |\n| 3 | 14 |\n| 4 | 18 |\n| 5 | 22 |\n\n(a) Draw a graph of position against time with a fitted line. [5]\n(b) Calculate the gradient, showing its construction on your graph. [3]\n(c) Explain whether position is directly proportional to elapsed time, using the data or graph. [2]','(a) Time / s is horizontal and position / cm vertical. Use uniform scales occupying at least half the grid, plot all six points and fit the line through the trend. The line crosses the position axis at 2 cm.\n(b) A triangle between (0 s, 2 cm) and (5 s, 22 cm) gives a rise of 20 cm and a run of 5 s; 20 ÷ 5 = 4 cm/s.\n(c) It is not direct proportionality to the stated elapsed time: the line has a nonzero position intercept. Equal changes in equal time instead support a constant rate of position change.',['Graph: axes with quantity/unit (1), suitable scales (1), all six points (2), fitted line (1).','Gradient: visible large construction (1), slope calculation (1), axis-ratio unit (1).','Conclusion: correct relationship judgement (1), supporting intercept evidence (1).','This is an original practice method and table; the full investigation-plan exemplar is specified in the work order.'],kind='practical_task',bloom='Apply')]
